---
import LearningGuideLayout from "../../layouts/LearningGuideLayout.astro";
import GuideFigure from "../../components/GuideFigure.astro";
---

<LearningGuideLayout
  title="How to Make a Playable Godot Level in Blender"
  description="Build and export a correctly scaled, collidable Blender level, add an FPE player, and prove the complete playable Blender-to-Godot workflow."
  summary="Do not begin with a giant dream level. Begin with one charming room you can walk around. That tiny success proves that your art, collision, player, metadata, export, and Godot runtime all understand one another."
  relatedSlugs={[
    "create-player-in-blender-for-godot",
    "blender-to-godot-gameplay-import",
    "instant-game-mechanics",
  ]}
  godotSource={{
    label: "Model export considerations",
    href: "https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/assets_pipeline/importing_3d_scenes/model_export_considerations.html",
  }}
  fpeHeading="What FPE adds to the level"
>
  <div class="guide-summary">
    <strong>Before decorating:</strong> give the ground collision with Godot's <code
      >-col</code
    > name suffix, select the player marker, and check <strong>Player</strong> in
    Blender's <strong>Item → Folded Paper Engine</strong> panel. Those two steps
    turn a room you can see into a level you can stand in and play.
  </div>
  <GuideFigure
    src="/assets/images/guides/blender/player-marker-checkbox.png"
    alt="Blender Item sidebar with the FPE Player checkbox enabled on a selected marker"
    caption="In Blender, select the marker and enable Player under Item → Folded Paper Engine."
    placement="panel"
  />
  <h2>Establish the coordinate contract</h2><p>
    Godot uses Y up and forward along negative Z for cameras; glTF defines
    consistent asset orientation between tools. Work at real scale, apply
    transforms where appropriate, and verify one known-size object before
    building a whole map. For oriented assets, test forward direction early
    rather than repairing every character later.
  </p>
  <h2>A practical level workflow</h2><ol>
    <li>
      In Blender, block the walkable spaces with simple geometry and test scale
      in Godot immediately.
    </li><li>
      Name the floor mesh with the <code>-col</code> suffix so Godot imports a visible
      mesh with static collision. Watch <a
        href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=MNUCt67IlXE&list=PLM1XlwxBilobr6XVs-7H6O_mMXAiHZEcz"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">Collision (Walls &amp; Floors)</a
      > for the complete walkthrough.
    </li><li>
      Add a simple player marker, then check <strong>Player</strong> in Blender's
      FPE panel. The <a href="/guides/create-player-in-blender-for-godot.html"
        >player guide</a
      > and <a
        href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JlcHwvsJcNU&list=PLM1XlwxBilobr6XVs-7H6O_mMXAiHZEcz"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">Player video</a
      > show every setting.
    </li><li>
      Choose stable, readable names for rooms, entrances, spawn points, and
      interactive props.
    </li><li>
      Apply or deliberately preserve transforms before export. Be consistent
      across reusable assets.
    </li><li>
      Export GLB with <strong>Custom Properties</strong> enabled. Follow the <a
        href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hDyJ5Th2-84&list=PLM1XlwxBilobr6XVs-7H6O_mMXAiHZEcz"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer">FPE Export video</a
      > if this is your first trip into Godot.
    </li><li>
      Re-export often. The workflow only becomes trustworthy by surviving
      ordinary iteration.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <GuideFigure
    src="/assets/images/guides/blender/gltf-custom-properties-export.png"
    alt="Blender glTF export dialog with Include expanded and Custom Properties enabled"
    caption="Before exporting GLB, expand Include and enable Custom Properties so FPE's authored settings travel with the scene."
  />
  <GuideFigure
    src="/assets/images/guides/godot/cookie-beans-runtime.png"
    alt="The FPE Cookie Beans demo running inside the Godot editor"
    caption="The practical test: run the imported level in Godot and verify that the player can stand and move in the scene."
  />
  <h2>Decide what belongs where</h2><ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Blender:</strong> geometry, transforms, materials, cameras, lights,
      animation, placement, and authored gameplay annotations.
    </li><li>
      <strong>Godot:</strong> game-wide managers, UI, save state, unique scripts,
      platform integration, and runtime-only effects.
    </li><li>
      <strong>Shared contract:</strong> stable IDs, event names, item kinds, scene
      paths, and feature types.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul>
    <li>
      Making manual edits inside an imported scene that disappear on reimport.
    </li><li>
      Using one giant level file when rooms have natural streaming or ownership
      boundaries.
    </li><li>
      Exporting unapplied negative scales or unpredictable triangulation.
    </li><li>Letting filenames and cross-scene IDs drift independently.</li>
  </ul>
  <Fragment slot="fpe"
    ><p>
      FPE makes Blender the authoring surface for common level mechanics as well
      as placement. Add triggers, speakers, players, inventory items, holdable
      props, scene settings, sub-scenes, and frame events through Blender
      panels. Export standard GLB and load it beneath one <code
        >FoldedPaperEngine</code
      > node. Whole-level and sub-scene commands keep re-exportable content separate
      from your stable Godot shell.
    </p><p>
      <a href="/docs/fpe-subscenes-and-levels.html"
        >Read the sub-scenes and level loading guide</a
      >.
    </p></Fragment
  >
</LearningGuideLayout>
